    "evidence_text": "Memory and Modularity in Cell-Fate Decision Making\n\nGenetically identical cells sharing an environment can display markedly different phenotypes.\n\nWe show that the motile state is 'memoryless', exhibiting no autonomous control over the time spent in the state.\n\nClathrin is required for the function of the mitotic spindle\n\nClathrin has an established function in the generation of vesicles that transfer membrane and proteins around the cell.\n\nClathrin bound to the spindle directly by the amino-terminal domain of clathrin heavy chain.\n\nHigh-Throughput Genetic Screens Identify a Large and Diverse Collection of New Sporulation Genes in Bacillus subtilis\n\nThe differentiation of the bacterium Bacillus subtilis into a dormant spore is among the most well-characterized developmental pathways in biology.\n\nMore recently, transcriptional profiling uncovered additional sporulation-induced genes required for successful spore development.\n\nOur screen identified 133 out of the 148 genes with known sporulation defects.\n\nThis analysis identified mutants that are delayed in the initiation of sporulation, defective in membrane remodeling, and impaired in spore maturation.\n\nSeveral mutants had novel sporulation phenotypes.\n\nWe performed in-depth characterization of two new factors that participate in cell-cell signaling pathways during sporulation.\n\nFinally, we discovered a large set of transposon insertions that trigger premature initiation of sporulation.\n\nMitosis is controlled by multiple kinases that drive cell cycle progression and prevent chromosome mis-segregation.\n\nThis is due to a crucial function of aurora C during these early embryonic cycles.\n\nThese defects correlate with abundant prometaphase figures and apoptotic cell death of the aurora B-deficient inner cell mass.\n\nFinally, aurora B-null cells partially arrest in the presence of nocodazole, suggesting that this kinase is not essential for the spindle assembly checkpoint.\n\nFine-tuning in regulation of Clp protein content in Bacillus subtilis.\n\nClp-controlled proteolysis in Bacillus subtilis seems to play a substantial role, particularly under stress conditions.\n\nCalibrated Western blot analyses were used to estimate the approximate numbers of heat-inducible Clp molecules within a single cell.\n\nGenome-wide identification of genes directly regulated by the pleiotropic transcription factor Spx in Bacillus subtilis\n\nIn many species, oocyte meiosis is carried out in the absence of centrioles.\n\nAs a result, microtubule organization, spindle assembly, and chromosome segregation proceed by unique mechanisms.\n\nMetabolic shutdown in Escherichia coli cells lacking the outer membrane channel TolC.\n\nMutational inactivation of TolC renders cells highly susceptible to antibiotics and leads to defects in secretion of protein toxins.\n\nDeficiency in l-serine deaminase results in abnormal growth and cell division of Escherichia coli K-12.\n\nWhen inoculated into Luria-Bertani (LB) broth with or without glucose, it makes very long filaments.\n\nThis result strongly indicated negative regulation of the clpC operon by one of its gene products.\n\nTwo-dimensional gel analysis and mRNA studies showed that the expression of other class III stress genes was at least partially influenced by the ctsR deletion.",
